A: I'm not entirely sure, but it seems that the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is not used correctly in your test. As per the Microsoft Docs, it is used on a parameter that represents the object being tested and PexMethod under test.

PexAssumeUnderTest Attribute
  Determines which object fields should be preserved in the mutant. In other
  words, fields of the objects passed as parameters to methods that are
  tested are mutated. At each mutant, fields that are marked with this
  attribute are preserved; fields for which PexMutate is used are
  mutated. 
In the example below, ArrayList instance is assumed-under-test:

[PexMethod]
[PexAssumeUnderTest("arrList")]
public void Add(ArrayList arrList, object o) {
    arrList.Add(o);
}

Therefore, if your PUT_ContainsContract method were implemented following the example, you would then have your arrList object preserved when generating fuzz test cases.
You might want to re-adjust your test as per the documentation so it becomes something like:
[PexMethodUnderTest]
public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest("arrList")]ArrayList arrList, int x)
--------------------            if (New_Ret == false) Assert.AreNotEqual(-1, Old_arrListCount);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the return value is true, the index values of the old and new lists are the same
            if (New_Ret == true) { Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX); }
           
        }
    }
}
```
By the above test, we have observed that the PexEngine automatically discovered and automatically generated several test cases for these contract properties. 

The PexEngine has automatically identified that the property Count will not be decremented if the Contains method returns false, and also that the IndexOf method will return a value that is not -1 when the Contains method returns true. Pex has also generated some cases where the IndexOf method will return -1 when Contains returns false which verifies that these are true properties of the underlying implementation.

Now, if you run the tests, Pex should find the following test cases automatically.

To validate or verify:
You can manually check that the contract was fulfilled or verify the behavior of the system under test by running the generated tests.

You will find that several test cases have been automatically generated by the PexEngine that verify all contract properties. All these test cases were identified and generated programmatically by the PexEngine.

In conclusion, Pex greatly increases the amount of testing that can be done on an application by automatically identifying and generating test cases for contracts and properties.

Pex can increase productivity by reducing the amount of time to produce tests and significantly reducing the amount of manual effort required. This has been verified in a number of industrial applications.


























































































































## Other .NET Code Contracts

- Code Contracts: Code contract can be applied to public, private, and protected methods
- Class Contracts: You can also put the code contracts at the class level. All the methods in these class contracts are also implicitly contracts.
- Contract Attributes: Contracts can be applied using attributes like Precondition, Postcondition, Requires, Ensures, Invariant, and AssumeNotNull and so on.
- ContractFailure: This will stop the execution of an operation if the contract fails. We can also ignore the contracts using the IgnoreOnContract.
- PexAssems: This attribute allows you to express assumptions about the method arguments or return values. The static method analysis tool then exploits these assumptions during the testing of the method.

## Conclusion

- In summary, Code Contracts provide a powerful way to express properties that an operation should maintain in its postconditions. It provides several different attributes to express those properties.
- You can use Code Contracts in C# at the method level, class level, and even at a block of code.
- Using these attributes, you can describe what the inputs and outputs should be and can verify at compile time that all preconditions are met before execution.
- Also postconditions hold that outputs (or return values) satisfy certain conditions, and class invariants describe the overall state of an object.
- Finally, Pex automatically generates unit tests that are based on contracts. It uses these to create sequences of execution that trigger possible branches in the method being tested.

