- https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/using-code-coverage-to-determine-how-much-code-is-being-tested?view=vs-2019
- https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/improve-code-quality?view=vs-2019
- https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/walkthrough-creating-a-unit-test-for-managed-code?view=vs-2--------------------	    if (x != Old_x && Old_arrListCount != -1) throw new System.ArgumentException("Assertion failed.\r\n @@@ If the return value is false, old list's count is not -1");
	}
    }
}

Now, I need to add PexSettings tag so it doesn't run the test with certain input values. I tried to put it into the [TestMethod] attribute, but it doesn't seem to work. Is there a way how to declare this within these method or the entire test class?

A: Actions such as PexObserve or PexSettings in TestMethod belong not within the TestMethod but in a separate [TestMethod] attribute. So, if you want to exclude input values, you should do this:
    [TestMethod]
    [DataSource("Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.DataSource.Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.DataSource.XML", "|DataDirectory|\\TestsCases.xml", "TestCase", DataAccessMethod.Sequential)]
    [PexSettings(MaxTestCount=100, UseReplayHarness=true, TestExcludePath="xxx, yyy")]
    public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexInSource(2)] ArrayList arrList, [PexInSource(1)] int x)
    { ... }

In this case, the TestExcludePath setting is used to exclude tests that match the regular expressions in the value of TestExcludePath. The regular expressions are matched against the full method signature (namespace + className + methodName).

If you want to exclude a specific method, the full method signature could be used as the regex. If you only want to exclude a few cases of a specific method, a line-based filter would be more suitable (that is more difficult to achieve).
For more information and for a full list of known limitations and potential improvements, please read the original post of this conversation.
Referenced conversation: In VS2010, How do you specify custom test parameters for Pex generated tests (using PexSettings attribute)
In Visual Studio Professional, CTP 2, PexSettings is not available as it is available in Visual Studio Premium.

A: You can use the PexSettings attribute to configure the Pex Engine:
[PexSettings(MaxTestCount=100, UseReplayHarness=true)]

For example, if you wanted to exclude a specific input, you can use the TestExcludePath parameter:
[PexSettings(MaxTestCount=100, UseReplayHarness=true, TestExcludePath="PUT_ContainsContract")]

That will exclude all test cases in PUT_ContainsContract.
For more information, see this post by Dan Rigby: http://blogs.msdn.com/b/dsplaid/archive/2009/01/15/pex-settings.aspx
Please note that some settings are not available in the CTP2 version of Pex (the free version at Visual Studio 2010). You might need to wait until the full version of Pex (the Professional version) to fully use all configuration options.

A: To filter the test with different combinations such as exclude certain types of data etc. you are better off using a DataSourceAttribute.
A more efficient approach would be:
[TestMethod]
[DataSource("Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.DataSource.XML",
    "|DataDirectory|\\TestCases.xml", 
    "TestCase",
    DataAccessMethod.Sequential)]
public void PUT_ContainsContract(ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
...
}

TestCases.xml may contain:
<TestCases>
  <TestCase>
    <Arraylist><Item>item1</Item><Item>item2</Item></ArrayList>
    <X>1</X>
  </TestCase>
  <TestCase>
    <Arraylist><Item>item1</Item><Item>item2</Item></ArrayList>
    <X>2</X>
  </TestCase>
</TestCases>

As for excluding certain test cases, you cannot do that directly in Pex settings because Pex settings are only designed to control the generation of tests, not to filter them. It's up to the test case developer to manage their test cases selection. So, instead of using Pex settings you will have to manage the selection of the test cases yourself based on your needs and requirements.
However, you can use the code below to exclude certain--------------------	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ the new list's Count is equal to old list's Count
        
        }
    }
}
```



This contract guarantees that if the element does not exist in the original list, it will remain true, and when the element does exist in the list, the return value from Contains method should be true.



Here we observe the elements count before and after adding the element to assure whether the count is changed which is a basic precondition for arrayList's Contains operation. 



We observe the index of the element before and after to assure whether the element is already in the arrayList. 



This contract ensures that the `ArrayList`'s `Contains` method behavior is observed correctly. If the return value from Contains method is false, the element was not added to the list. If the return value is true, the element was found in the list. The count and the indices of elements in the list are also observed.



It's a very comprehensive test for Contains method. 



Please notice that Pex does the exact path coverage and might not check every possible path due to its statistical nature. Therefore, it might miss some corner cases. 



To generate a comprehensive test, comprehensive input would be generated first and then the contracts would be generated respectively. 



After all the Pex tests passed, the developers can then test for actual behavior to ensure the contracts correctly represent the expected behavior.



In the end, we have covered the scenarios including edge cases for the Contains method. 

In the real world, the test cases may need to be refined according to actual need and test coverage goals.

**2.** **ArrayList/Contains method test case with Pex:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[HostType("Moles")]

public void TestWithPex()
{
 ArrayList list = new ArrayList {1, 2, 3, 4};
 bool result = list.Contains(2);
 Assert.IsTrue(result);
}
```

This test case verifies that Contains method returns true if the specified value is Present in the list/array. The method should return False for non existent element. 

### 5. Generated Inputs

To generate inputs for the `ArrayList` class, one can use Pex libraries or classes in Microsoft Fakes framework. The advantage of Pex in this situation is its ability to create a wide variety of tests that test through every possible code path of methods it annotates, which helps ensure that the unit tests consider all possible scenarios and edge-cases that the software program might encounter. 



For example, consider this class definition:

```csharp
public class MyList : ArrayList
{
    [PexMethod]
    public void PexMethodForTest(int value)
    {
        if (this.Contains(value))
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Value found in the list");
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Value not found in the list");
        }
    }
}
```

Generating inputs:

```csharp
PexInstanceFactory.Create<MyList>();
```

Pex creates numerous test inputs, and it generates a test method for each of these inputs, ensuring you have thorough code coverage. These generated tests cases may include positive and negative scenarios; both edge cases and middle values.

In this way, using Pex to generate appropriate, wide-ranging test cases in automated unit tests enables the developers to ensure code coverage, and that potential edge cases are also detected. This significantly aids in having robust, fully-functional unit testing systems. 

In a nutshell, Pex is an excellent tool to generate a wide variety of test inputs for your system and ensure code coverage. It can also handle scenarios and edge-cases, by creating numerous test inputs and generates test method accordingly. It plays a significant role in building more comprehensive and robust testing strategies. 

