A: This test case has an infinite branch due to the assumption of the ArrayList to be empty when being created. You stated:

[PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList = ArrayList.SortedArrayList();

If arrList is empty, this test case is essentially an infinite branch. The List.Contains method and associated index-of methods will always return false. The Pex test harness has no way of knowing this fact and hence it would fail this branch. Hence, it is recommended not to have anything under tests that could change the execution flow based on assumptions and preconditions. 

Also note that adding any item to the list (using Add method) could force the branch to execute even it wasn't supposed to due to the assumption. Therefore this branching is not acceptable. 

This is a general advice about designing test cases, never assume/precondition an object could only be in one state. And, if that is necessary, state the assumptions clear in the test case description which could help anyone reading it understand the intentions of the test case better.

Hope that helps, happy testing.

A: It's been a while since you asked this question, but I believe I found the error. You used AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true); which is not recommended by Microsoft Pex. It forces the entire test method to run, which means even if the conditions aren't met, you'll still get an infinite amount of combinations where it always passes.

Instead, you should use Assume.True(arrList.Count == 0); To check if it is truly empty. The assumption should be about the data, how it was initialized and not about the method results.

Furthermore, note that the test method PUT_ContainsContract should have [PexMethod] attribute instead of [TestMethod]. Furthermore, remove the [PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList = ArrayList.SortedArrayList(); because there's already a [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute.

It'll look like this:

```C# 
 [TestMethod]
 [HostType("Moles")]
 [PexMethod]
 public void PUT_ContainsContract( [PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
 {
     Assume.True(arrList.Count == 0);
     //...
 }
```
Hope that makes things clearer. I found your question really unclear and I'm happy to answer more questions if there is still any confusion.

A: It seems there is no actual logic in your PUT_ContainsContract method. This contract testing only performs some observations and assertions instead of actually performing a series of actions (add items, remove items, etc). 

It would be helpful to actually add some actions in this method and apply it to your [PexMethod] attribute to ensure the testing covers all functionality available in your code. For example:

```C#
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_ContainsContract(ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    Assume.True(arrList.Count == 0);

    arrList.Add(x);
    bool ret = arrList.Contains(x);

    arrList.Remove(x);
    bool ret2 = arrList.Contains(x);

    Assert.IsTrue(ret && !ret2);
}
```

In this example, we're assuming arrList is initially empty. We then add an item, check to see if it contained the item (should be true), remove the item, and then check again to see if it contained it (should be false). Assert checks both conditions and should return an error if one of them fails.

Remember this testing style is much more effective when you're testing your contracts/logic directly. This will provide more coverage and give a clearer understanding of how your code is functioning. It usually does a--------------------
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ If the return value is true and old list's count is not -1, then new list's count should be one greater than the old list's count

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ If the return value is true, both New arrList's IndexOf(x) and LastIndexOf(x) should be the same and should be greater than Old arrList's IndexOf(x)

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ If the return value is true, New arrList's LastIndexOf(x) should be the same or greater than Old arrList's LastIndexOf(x)
        }
    }
}

This is my partial code. I have annotated it in a way for the Pex framework to verify and I have some assertions to check properties of the ArrayList such as count. 
I have an important question:
I want to check the array list count before and after invoking a method. How to do that?
Do I have to make PexObserve calls for each of those?
Thank you.

A: You can use PexObserve to keep track of a variable's value over multiple method calls. The PexObserve.ValueForViewing("name", value); line will store the name and value of the variable you are interested in for inspection in the Pex explorer.
In your case, yes, you would need to make one PexObserve call for the arrList.Count property before and after your method was called. When Pex comes across the PexObserve call, it will track the value of the variable.
You will need to insert this inside your method's PexAssumeUnderTest test method. For instance:
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$arrList_Count_Before_Method", arrList.Count);
Your_Method(arrList, x);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$arrList_Count_After_Method", arrList.Count);

In this way, when you run Pex, it will show you the values of arrList.Count before and after your method was called, which will help you establish the properties of the ArrayList.
Bear in mind that Pex will provide you with test cases, but they cannot predict or verify what your method does. The test case is to show you that your method will behave as expected, providing you an input set to consider while writing additional assertions.
To use PexObserve, make sure your code compiles correctly and you have all the required references.

A: PexObserve will help you see values and help understand what happened to the collection during your call. The basic usage is:
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("variableName", variableValue);
In this line, you're telling Pex to save the name and value of the variable you're interested in for viewing later.
For example, you can observe after you have called clear function:
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("arrListCountAfterClear", arrList.Count);

However, PexObserve values won't provide you the change in Count during single method call, unless you make additional observe points in the same method.
If you want to verify the change in count in single method call, it seems like you need to write additional assertions that would make sense for your method. For instance, if your method adds an item and you are interested in size of list, you would have to write something like this:
Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);

This requires that you have the old size prior to your method call, and the new size post method call to verify the change.

A: I'm not sure why you don't see PexObserve calls for the count before and after methods. The count is an important property that can be checked in most scenarios but as far as I know, Pex doesn't track it automatically. 
Remember that Pex is designed to explore your algorithm, it isn't designed to measure your concrete implementation details such as the ArrayList implementation.
Here is an example of how to make PexObserve calls in the part of your code:
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$arrList_Count_Before_Method", arrList.Count);
YourMethod(testValue);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$arrList_Count_After_Method", arrList.Count);

