# <a name="ca2229-implement-the-correct-constructors"></a>CA2229: Implementar los constructores de serialización correctos
[CA2229: Implementar constructores de serialización correctos](https://docs.microsoft.com/visualstudio/code-quality/ca22--------------------	    Debug.Assert( !New_Ret | (Old_arrListIndexOfX ==  -1 && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1 && Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount ));
        }
    }
}

I want to test the following case:
Suppose the array list already contain {10, 20, 30, 40, 50} 
I want to add the number 20 to the list and check the contains method, to make sure  the number 20 is still in the list.

A: When you create the list in the setup, you will add 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 to it. Then, you call the PUT_ContainsContract method with x = 20, which will check if the list contains 20. The contains method should return true since 20 is supposed to be in the list.

A: [PexMethod]
[PexExploration]
public void ContainsTest([PexAssumeNotNull] ArrayList list, int x)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(list.Contains(x));
}

You should add an array list to this test method and the number you want to test. The test verifies that the ArrayList contains the number that you specified. If it does contain it, the test will pass. If the number isn't in the ArrayList, the test will fail. If you make a change that breaks a test, you will be able to see the line of code that caused the failure. This way you can then fix the bug.
Also be mindful that using ArrayList will be slow, especially if you are dealing with large amounts of data. It would be more optimized to use List<> or some other kind of collection. 
Note: ArrayList is obsolete, consider using List<T> or other alternatives.
This is just an example how you can test ArrayList's Contains method. You might need to adjust it according to your actual requirements. Please replace 'T' with int if you are dealing only with integers.

A: First off, Contains would not work on a list containing int objects. Because your list is populated with int values, you'll need to box them first. The below test method will help you test whether an item already exists in a list, assuming that the list is not empty.
[PexMethod]
public void ContainsTest(List<int> list, int item)
{
    Assume.True(list.Count > 0);

    var result = list.Contains(item);

    Assert.IsTrue(result);
}

Here, for parameter "list", you might specify a known set of int values in your tests (e.g. {10, 20, 30}).
And for parameter "item", you may want to consider creating a range that includes all existing items (e.g., [PexRange(10, 30)]), and the remaining items (e.g., [PexRange(50, 200) and so on according to your array).
This creates a test with several "runs" and checks every item for existence in the list. The assumption is that the list count must be more than zero. A positive outcome means that the item you've passed is in the list. Negative results imply that the item you've passed is not in the list.
For example, executing the test with a list containing {10, 20, 30}, and item 20 will result to pass. If you will run that test again with item 40, it will fail, because 40 is not in the list.
Please also note, always ensure the [PexExploration] attribute is also used when you apply Fuzzing techniques. Fuzzing will increase the number and variety of test inputs that are fed into your test procedures.
A good practice to follow is test edge cases, as well as different type of inputs. This helps your tests to be more reliable and effective. 
Note
The "Assume" class in the sample is part of the Visual Studio ALM (Application Lifecycle Management) Test extension and provides support for parameterized and exploration test generation. It only runs the test if the provided argument is met by the method.

A: The ArrayList.Contains method only works with reference equality, not with value equality. This means that it will only return true for the exact same object instance.
In your case, you add int values to the ArrayList and then try to use Contains to look for them. This won't work as you would expect because Contains uses reference equality, not value--------------------